{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/786097-vtr-2nd-ed-vampire-history","id":786097,"title":"VtR 2nd Ed Vampire History","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-12-14T11:35:06-08:00","id":786097,"title":"VtR 2nd Ed Vampire History","content":"So in VtR the Fog of Eternity made certain that history is all a fog. But what about in 2nd? I don't see vampire history in the book much.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4135-blue-thomas","name":"Blue Thomas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4135_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-14T11:45:09-08:00","id":786103,"title":"","content":"Because vampires lie, don't tend to write things down and often burn them when they do. Then there's the Strix destroying groups of Kindred many times.<br /> <br /> Think about how uncertain history <i>actually is </i>anyway, even stuff that has occurred within living memory. On top of that, while most vampires don't lose their memories, some still do, and they're also going to be adding their voices to the chorus. The Lancea et Sanctum covenant writeup mentions their tendency to collect histories on the Kindred, and to burn the ones they don't agree with. It's a <i>secret </i>history too, since Kindred don't happen to actually advertise themselves all that well. So all that said, it's pretty easy to recognize why Kindred history would theoretically be fragmented.<br /> <br /> Now, in contrast, we have the Dead Clans and Covenants in the very first section, which might not actually accompany <i>dates </i>but actually give huge implications for what parts of the setting are like. See: The Gallow's Post or the Legion of the Dead or the Julii.<br /> <br /> <i>Second</i>, we get the settings around the world. Those give you histories of particular domains, which also imply history for the regions that surround them, so that's <i>another </i>source of 2e history.<br /> <br /> <i>Third, </i>in the Birds of Dis section, it mentions a number of flashpoints in history when a huge parliament of Strix appeared and screwed stuff up. For example, there's the Fall of the Camarilla (Who are confirmed as part of the canon still), they're also mentioned to have been at the destruction of the library of Alexandria, another place where Kindred were presumably in conflict with them. Perhaps most significantly, they are also mentioned to have actually <i>warred </i>with Kindred in Transylvania. All these references are together at the start.<br /> <br /> If that's not enough history for you, then you can jump beyond the core book and look at the <i>Dark Eras </i>kickstarter manuscript. In Requiem for Regina, there's a whole lot of details that hint at the history of the British isles vis a vis the Kindred, <i>and </i>it gives you like, a couple centuries of history <i>after </i>the setting takes place.<br /> <br /> Furthermore, in the final version of Dark Eras, it will feature (brief!) sidebars for <i>every splat </i>in each Dark Era, giving a very short sentence or two discussing what's going on. <i>AND </i>we're getting the Black Death, a crossover setting with Vampires and Prometheans, in which the Morbus Bloodline (founded all the way back in Rome!) should make an appearance.<br /> <br /> So in short, there's actually a <i>lot </i>of history out there in the books already if you look for it, and I can only imagine books like <i>A Thousand Years of Night </i>will contain even more, though most likely not to the degree that 1e's <i>Ancient Mysteries </i>gives us.<br /> <br /> I hope that helps!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-14T13:37:07-08:00","id":786192,"title":"","content":"I'm still reading the book as for the other one I haven't read it yet .","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4135-blue-thomas","name":"Blue Thomas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4135_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-14T14:40:35-08:00","id":786264,"title":"","content":"You shouldn't expect much more else in &quot;canonical&quot; setting's history, though. The end of the Fog of Eternity as an unavoidable thing means that the ST can wright up some history for the chronicle, but I doubt they will make too much official material in this regard. This is, still, a toolbox setting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-14T14:44:25-08:00","id":786269,"title":"","content":"There is the Dark Eras, though vampire only got one and a half of those, I believe.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-14T15:55:10-08:00","id":786310,"title":"","content":"Vampires haven't had anything resembling a cohesive central authority in nearly two thousand years, and instead have to rely on the individuals around them and the biased cults they belong to for any information about the past. Even without the Fog, memory is one of the slipperiest things around; if a human being can only remember the most important moments from a decade earlier in his life, how much can a half-mad immortal predator truly recall from five centuries ago?<br /> <br /> Requiem 1e had the Fog so that individual Storytellers could write whatever history they wanted for vampires, because that's a central tenet of the nWoD/CofD.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-14T16:18:37-08:00","id":786324,"title":"","content":"The Covanent Books are another good place to go for Vamprie history. I'll see if I can't whip up a timeline in Aeon Flux, although that is likely to take a couple of days.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/>